SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);

INSERT INTO `mmd_blog_post` (`title`, `url_key`, `excerpt`, `content`, `author`, `status`, `published_at`, `related_skus`, `source_sku`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `meta_keywords`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) SELECT 'SEO in the Age of AI Overviews: How to Get Cited by Google''s Generative Answers', 'generative-ai-seo-google-ai-overview-optimization', 'Google''s AI Overviews now answer a large share of searches before anyone clicks a blue link. That does not kill SEO — it changes what winning looks like: being the source the AI cites, not the result it summarises past. Here is what actually changes, the on-page structure that gets you quoted, how to use generative AI for keyword research and content production without publishing thin slop, and how to measure visibility when impressions stop turning into clicks.', '<p><strong>Short version:</strong> AI Overviews have not deleted search traffic — they have re-priced it. Informational queries that used to send you a click now get answered on the results page, while the queries that still convert are the ones where a human wants to compare, buy, or verify. The winning move is to become <em>the cited source inside the answer</em> and to shift your content mix toward the queries an AI summary cannot satisfy. That takes a different page structure, a different keyword process, and a different measurement habit — all three are learnable in a couple of days.</p> <p>This post covers what actually changed, the page structure that earns citations, how to use generative AI across the SEO workflow without publishing thin content that Google''s helpful-content systems demote, and what to track once impressions stop converting into clicks at the old rate.</p> <h2>What AI Overviews actually changed</h2> <p>An AI Overview is a generated summary that sits above the organic results for a subset of queries, with links to the sources it drew on. Three consequences matter for anyone doing SEO in Singapore right now:</p> <ul> <li><strong>The click, not the ranking, is what got scarcer.</strong> You can hold position 3 and still lose sessions, because the answer was delivered before the user scrolled. Ranking reports that only track position now overstate your health.</li> <li><strong>Citation is the new position one.</strong> Being one of the linked sources inside the Overview puts your brand in front of the user at the exact moment they get the answer. That is a branded-search and trust play even when it is not an immediate click.</li> <li><strong>Query intent got sorted for you.</strong> Definitional, "what is", and single-fact queries increasingly resolve on-page. Comparison, pricing, local, transactional and "show me how, with proof" queries still send traffic — and they are where commercial value lives anyway.</li> </ul> <p>The strategic read: stop competing for the queries an AI answers in two sentences, and invest in the ones that need evidence, opinion, first-hand experience, local specificity or a decision. That is not a workaround — it is the same advice that has been correct since the helpful-content update, now with sharper teeth.</p> <h2>How to get cited by a generative answer</h2> <p>Generative systems pull passages, not pages. A page that ranks well but buries its answer inside a 300-word preamble is far harder to quote than one that answers cleanly and then elaborates. Practical structure that works:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Answer in the first 40–60 words, under a heading that matches the question.</strong> Use the question form as an <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> and give a direct, self-contained answer immediately below. If the paragraph only makes sense after reading the previous section, it cannot be extracted.</li> <li><strong>Write extractable chunks.</strong> One idea per paragraph, tables for comparisons, ordered lists for procedures. A step-by-step with real numbered steps is far more quotable than the same steps as prose.</li> <li><strong>Add the things a model cannot invent.</strong> Original data, screenshots of your own process, prices, dates, Singapore-specific rules, named tools with versions. Generative answers preferentially cite pages carrying specifics that are not already in the model''s head.</li> <li><strong>Mark it up.</strong> <code>Article</code>, <code>FAQPage</code>, <code>HowTo</code>, <code>Product</code> and <code>Organization</code> schema make your entities and answers machine-legible. Schema will not force a citation, but it removes ambiguity about what your page asserts.</li> <li><strong>Make authorship and freshness real.</strong> A named author with credentials, a genuine last-updated date, and citations to primary sources are exactly the signals both the ranking systems and the summarisers lean on.</li> <li><strong>Keep answers crawlable in plain HTML.</strong> If the answer text only appears after a JavaScript interaction, assume it will not be extracted. Native <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> accordions keep FAQ answers in the DOM; a JS accordion that renders on click may not.</li> </ol> <p>A useful test: paste your page into a chatbot and ask it to answer the target query using only that page. If it struggles, or answers from general knowledge instead of your text, the page is not extractable — fix the structure before touching link building.</p> <h2>Using generative AI across the SEO workflow</h2> <p>The other half of "generative AI for SEO" is using the tools to do the work. Where they genuinely earn their keep:</p> <h3>Keyword research and clustering</h3> <p>Export your Search Console queries, then have an AI cluster them by intent rather than by string match — informational, comparison, transactional, navigational. That clustering is the input to the strategic decision above: which clusters are being eaten by AI Overviews, and which still deserve a page. Ask it to draft the questions a real buyer would ask at each stage; those become your <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>s.</p> <h3>Briefs and outlines, not finished drafts</h3> <p>AI is excellent at producing a structured brief — target query, intent, competing pages, headings, entities to cover, questions to answer, internal links to include. It is much weaker at producing publishable prose that carries first-hand experience. Use it for the brief and the skeleton; supply the specifics, the opinion and the evidence yourself.</p> <h3>On-page optimisation at scale</h3> <p>Title tags and meta descriptions across hundreds of URLs, alt text for image libraries, internal-link suggestions from your own sitemap, schema JSON-LD generation, and rewriting a wall of prose into extractable chunks — all of this is high-volume, rules-based work that AI does well and humans do slowly.</p> <h3>Technical and content audits</h3> <p>Feed a crawl export in and ask for pattern analysis: orphan pages, thin clusters, cannibalising URLs, missing schema, inconsistent canonicals. The model will not run the crawl, but it is fast at spotting patterns in the output.</p> <h2>The trap: thin AI content at scale</h2> <p>Google''s stated position is that it rewards helpful, people-first content regardless of how it was produced — and that using automation primarily to manipulate rankings is spam. Both halves are true and people keep hearing only one. The failure mode we see most often is a site that publishes eighty generated articles in a month, gets an initial impressions bump, then flatlines or falls, because every page says exactly what every competitor''s generated page says.</p> <p>Guardrails that keep AI-assisted content on the right side of that line:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Every page must contain something only you could write</strong> — your data, your screenshots, your client outcome, your local pricing, your considered opinion.</li> <li><strong>A named human reviews and edits before publish</strong>, and is accountable for factual accuracy. Verify every statistic and every citation the model produces; fabricated sources are still the most common and most damaging failure.</li> <li><strong>Publish fewer, deeper pages.</strong> One genuinely definitive page beats ten interchangeable ones, and it is the deep page that gets cited.</li> <li><strong>Never auto-publish.</strong> Generation is a drafting step, not a publishing pipeline.</li> </ul> <h2>Measuring visibility when clicks decouple from rankings</h2> <p>If your reporting is "average position" and "clicks", AI Overviews will make your work look worse than it is. Add these:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Impressions vs clicks by query cluster</strong> in Search Console. Rising impressions with falling CTR on informational clusters is the AI Overview signature — and it is a signal to reallocate, not to panic.</li> <li><strong>Citation tracking.</strong> Spot-check your priority queries for whether an Overview appears and whether you are among the linked sources. Several rank trackers now report this; a manual sample of 20 queries a month is enough to see the trend.</li> <li><strong>Branded search volume.</strong> The clearest evidence that being cited without being clicked still pays.</li> <li><strong>Assisted conversions and direct traffic.</strong> Users who see you in an answer often arrive later by name or direct.</li> <li><strong>Referrals from AI assistants.</strong> Check your analytics for referrers from ChatGPT, Perplexity and Copilot — small today for most Singapore sites, growing, and a direct read on whether your content is being surfaced by assistants.</li> </ul> <h2>A 30-day plan you can actually run</h2> <ol> <li><strong>Week 1 — diagnose.</strong> Pull 12 months of Search Console data, cluster queries by intent, and identify which clusters lost CTR while holding impressions. Those are your AI-Overview-affected pages.</li> <li><strong>Week 2 — restructure your top 10 pages.</strong> Question-form headings, a direct answer in the first 60 words of each section, comparison tables, real numbered steps, FAQ schema. No new content — just make the existing answers extractable.</li> <li><strong>Week 3 — add what only you have.</strong> Original data, screenshots, pricing, local context, a named author bio. Update the last-modified dates honestly.</li> <li><strong>Week 4 — build the workflow.</strong> Write your brief-generation prompt, your on-page-optimisation prompt and your audit prompt, and save them. Set up the new measurement dashboard so month two is comparable.</li> </ol> <p>None of this is exotic. It is the discipline of answering a real question better than anyone else, expressed in a structure that a machine can read — with generative AI doing the volume work so you can spend your time on the parts that make the page worth citing.</p> <h2>Learn this properly, with funding</h2> <p>Reading the method gets you the concept; running it on your own site with a trainer correcting your prompts and your page structure gets you the skill. The direct route is <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-generative-ai-for-search-engine-optimization-seo.html"><strong>WSQ Generative AI for Search Engine Optimization (SEO)</strong></a> — a 2-day, 16-hour beginner-level course covering SEO and generative AI fundamentals, AI-powered keyword research and content strategy, creating SEO-optimised content with generative AI, AI for on-page optimisation, and SEO performance, ethics and best practices.</p> <p>Because it is a WSQ course, the funding is substantial for eligible learners:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Full fee $750</strong> before GST.</li> <li><strong>$442.50 nett</strong> for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above (50% funded).</li> <li><strong>$292.50 nett</strong> for Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above under MCES, and for eligible SMEs (70% funded).</li> <li><strong>SkillsFuture Credit, PSEA and UTAP</strong> can offset the fee payable after funding, employers can tap <strong>SFEC</strong> and claim <strong>Absentee Payroll</strong>.</li> </ul> <p>Related courses if you want a different entry point: <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/generative-ai-for-seo.html">Generative AI for SEO</a> (non-WSQ, shorter, no funding paperwork), <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-enhancing-online-presence-with-ai-powered-search-engine-optimization-seo.html">WSQ Enhancing Online Presence with AI-Powered SEO</a>, and <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-agentic-ai-for-digital-marketing.html">WSQ Agentic AI for Digital Marketing</a> if you want the automation layer on top. Browse the full <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/search-engine-optimisation-seo-training-courses.html">SEO course listing</a>, the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-generative-ai-courses.html">WSQ Generative AI courses</a> and <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/content-marketing-courses.html">Content Marketing courses</a> to see every option and the next available dates.</p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is SEO dead because of AI Overviews?</summary><div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No, but the scoreboard changed. Zero-click informational queries are genuinely harder to monetise, while comparison, transactional, local and evidence-heavy queries still send valuable traffic — and being cited inside an AI Overview is itself a visibility win that drives branded search later. What is dead is the strategy of ranking thin pages for definitional keywords and hoping for clicks.</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">Will Google penalise me for using AI to write content?</summary><div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Google''s stated position is that it rewards helpful, people-first content however it was produced, and treats automation used primarily to manipulate rankings as spam. In practice that means AI-assisted content with genuine expertise, original data and human review performs fine, while mass-generated interchangeable pages get demoted. The differentiator is substance, not the tool.</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">How do I know if a query I care about triggers an AI Overview?</summary><div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>The cheapest method is to search it manually in an incognito window set to Singapore and record what appears — sample 20 priority queries monthly and track the trend. Several commercial rank trackers now flag AI Overview presence and whether your domain is among the cited sources, which is worth paying for once you are managing more than a handful of pages.</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">What is the single highest-impact change to make first?</summary><div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Restructure your top pages so every section opens with a direct, self-contained answer of about 40 to 60 words under a question-form heading. It costs nothing, requires no new content, and it is the change that most often flips a page from "summarised past" to "cited".</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need technical or coding skills to take the WSQ course?</summary><div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. It is a beginner-level course; the entry requirement is basic computer literacy, a minimum of 3 GCE ''O'' Level passes including English or WPL Level 5, at least one year of working experience and a minimum age of 18. You need a Windows or Mac laptop — no software purchase is required.</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">How much will the WSQ SEO course actually cost me?</summary><div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>The full fee is $750 before GST. Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above pay $442.50 nett after 50% funding, and Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above pay $292.50 nett under MCES (70% funding), as do eligible SMEs. You can then use SkillsFuture Credit, PSEA or UTAP to offset the amount payable, and employers can claim Absentee Payroll. Note that promo codes cannot be applied to WSQ courses.</p></div></details> </div> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-generative-ai-for-search-engine-optimization-seo.html"><strong>View WSQ Generative AI for Search Engine Optimization (SEO) — dates, funding and registration →</strong></a></p>', 'Tertiary Infotech Academy', 1, '2025-11-04', 'TGS-2020503501', 'TGS-2020503501', 'Generative AI for SEO: How to Rank in Google''s AI Overviews (2025)', 'AI Overviews answer searches before the click. Learn the page structure that gets you cited, how to use generative AI for keyword research and on-page SEO without thin content, and how to measure visibility when impressions stop becoming clicks. WSQ course funding from $292.50.', 'generative AI for SEO, Google AI Overview optimization, AI Overviews SEO, AI search optimization Singapore, WSQ SEO course, generative engine optimization, AI content strategy, SEO training Singapore', NOW(), NOW() FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg = 1 AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM `mmd_blog_post` WHERE `url_key` = 'generative-ai-seo-google-ai-overview-optimization');

